==English==
{{wikipedia}}
[[File:Eighth notes and rest.svg|thumb|An eighth note with stem facing up, an eighth note with stem facing down, and a quaver rest.]]

===Etymology===
From {{inh|en|enm|quaveren}}, frequentative form of {{m|enm|quaven}}, {{m|enm|cwavien||to tremble}}, equivalent to {{suffix|en|quave|er|id2=verbal frequentative}}. Cognate with {{cog|nds|quabbeln||to quiver}}, {{cog|de|quabbeln}}, {{m|de|quappeln||to quiver}}. More at {{l|en|quave}}, {{l|en|quab}}, {{l|en|quiver}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ˈkweɪvə(ɹ)/}}
** {{audio|en|LL-Q1860 (eng)-Vealhurl-quaver.wav|a=Southern England}}
* {{rhymes|en|eɪvə(ɹ)|s=2}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# A [[trembling]] shake.
# A [[trembling]] of the [[voice]], as in [[speaking]] or [[singing]].
# {{lb|en|music}} an [[eighth note]], drawn as a [[crotchet]] ([[quarter note]]) with a tail.
#* {{RQ:Mansfield Bliss|pages=140–141|pageref=141|passage=The crotchets and '''quavers''' are dancing up and down the stave like little black boys on a fence.}}

